7. Investigation and Explanation
Why does input size alone fail to describe computational effort?
Input size alone does not determine the execution time of a backtracking algorithm.

The structure and connections between vertices also affect the amount of searching.
- Graph A has no edges, so every colour assignment is valid.
- Graph B contains a triangle, which creates a colouring conflict.
- The algorithm must explore different assignments before identifying that no solution exists.
- Therefore, Graph B requires more search nodes and backtracking operations.
Which structural property caused additional search?
The main reason is the placement of the conflicting triangle after seven isolated vertices.

The seven isolated vertices allow the algorithm to explore many possible colour combinations without detecting any conflict.

When the algorithm finally reaches the triangle, it discovers that two colours are insufficient.

As a result, it must return to earlier vertices and try different assignments.
Why does pruning occur later in Graph B?
The feasibility test checks only whether the current vertex conflicts with an already coloured neighbour.

The first seven vertices have no neighbours, so every assignment passes the feasibility test.

The conflict becomes visible only when the algorithm reaches the triangle.

This delayed conflict detection causes additional backtracking.
8. Additional Investigation – Effect of Vertex Ordering
An interesting observation can be made by running Graph B using Version 2 (highest-degree-first heuristic) from Task B1.

The three triangle vertices have the highest degree, so the algorithm selects them first.

